Mull names a mutant by its mutator and source range, so two mutations of
one statement, the destructor of a temporary on the normal path and the
same destructor in the exception-cleanup landing pad, or two instantiations
of one template, share a name; the trampoline of a function checks that one
name for each of its clones and dispatches the last it registered, so the
others never run and are reported under the one that did. The identifier
gains a seventh part, a hash of the function's mangled name and an ordinal
among the function's mutants of the same range, so every clone runs and is
reported on its own; the runner reads the first six parts as before and the
replacement from the last. A function's hash and ordinals are the same in
every translation unit that instantiates it, so a linkonce copy dropped at
link time still shares its identifiers with the copy kept.

